      <h1>{frontmatter.title}</h1>
      <time>{frontmatter.date}</time>


<p class="intro">Pandemic vs Forbidden Island is the same designer asking the same question twice, and the answer he gave each time is different. Both are cooperative. Both are Matt Leacock. One costs $44.43, the other $20.99.</p>

<p>If you want the answer with no scrolling: buy <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Forbidden+Island+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Forbidden Island</a> if you are teaching a table that has never played a cooperative game, or you want to spend $21 instead of $44. Buy <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Pandemic+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Pandemic</a> if your group already plays games and wants the version with more to hold in your head.</p>

<p>Both play 2 to 4 players. Both are in my collection. The difference is not difficulty in the sense of "harder to win." It is how many things you have to track at once, and that decides which table each one belongs on.</p>

<h2 id="same">What They Share</h2>

<p>Start with how much is genuinely identical, because it is more than people expect.</p>

<p>Both are cooperative: everyone wins or everyone loses, and there is no traitor. Both put you in a fixed number of roles with a special ability. Both run on a deck that acts as a clock, and in both games that clock is the real opponent. Both scale on the same dial, which is how many cards the clock burns.</p>

<p>Both are also quarterback-able. That is the standard complaint about cooperative games and it is honest here: one player who knows the game well can run the whole table if you let them. Forbidden Island makes that harder than Pandemic does, for a reason worth knowing.</p>

<h2 id="tracking">The Number That Separates Them</h2>

<p>Pandemic asks you to track four diseases, each with a cure and an eradication state. You are managing a hand of cards toward four separate sets, watching two separate loss conditions, and moving around a map that connects in different ways in different cities.</p>

<p>Forbidden Island asks you to collect four treasures and get everyone off the island. It has one loss condition you have to watch in two places, and the island itself is a shrinking map you can see at a glance.</p>

<p>That is the whole comparison. Pandemic has roughly twice as many things in the air, and its complexity rating reflects that: 2.5 for Pandemic against 1.7 for Forbidden Island, on the standard scale where most family games sit under 2.</p>

