<h2 id="shelf">The Shelf Test</h2>

<p>Here is the test I use when a game is this close on paper. Which one do you reach for when the table is mixed, some people know the game and some do not?</p>

<p>Forbidden Island wins that. The board tells everyone what is happening. You can see the island sinking, you can see which tiles are flooded, and a first-time player can make a real decision without being told what the right move is. The 30-minute play time means a loss does not cost you the evening, so losing does not create the tension that makes a group blame each other.</p>

<p>Pandemic loses that test, and it is not a flaw. The four-disease board is exactly what experienced players want: more to weigh, more ways a turn can matter, and a loss you can usually trace back to one specific decision. That is a better game for a group that plays together regularly. It is a worse game for a group that is learning together.</p>

<p>One honest thing: the quarterback problem is worse in Pandemic because there is more to be right about. If you are the person who sees the optimal line, you have to actively shut up, or you will play the game by yourself with three spectators.</p>

<h2 id="pick">Where Each One Belongs</h2>

<p><strong>Buy Forbidden Island ($20.99)</strong> if you are introducing cooperative games, playing with kids around 10, or you want a 30-minute game that fits after dinner. At this price it is the cheapest real co-op in the hobby, and it shares a designer with the one everyone recommends.</p>

<p><strong>Buy Pandemic ($44.43)</strong> if your group already enjoys heavier games and wants a co-op with a real chance of a memorable loss. It is the game that made cooperative play mainstream for a reason, and 7.6 against 7.1 is a fair gap.</p>

<p>Buy both eventually. Forbidden Island is the teaching game, Pandemic is the next step, and together they cost less than one mid-weight strategy box. To see Pandemic in play, <a href="/blog/how-to-play-pandemic">how to play Pandemic</a> walks the setup and the outbreak rules. If you want more cooperative options, <a href="/blog/best-cooperative-board-games">the best cooperative board games</a> lists the ones worth owning, and <a href="/game/pandemic">Pandemic</a> and <a href="/game/forbidden-island">Forbidden Island</a> both have full specs in the game database.</p>


